In the object oriented world of thinking you have objects that are made of classes. These classes can be ordered through a special inheritance relationship into hierarchies of parent/child relationships. 

Upcasting means taking a reference to an object and changing
its type to that of any of its parent classes. (for java, add to this list also all the interfaces that any of the parent classes may be implementing)

For example:
class A extends B{
}
class B{
}
class Program{

    .......
    B b = new B();
    A a = (A) b; // this is upcasting!
    .......
}

Upcasting in java is implicit. This means that you dont need
the preceding parenthesis with the upcasting type.
    A a = b;
would have equally worked. This is because you are going from something specific (class B - child) to something more general (class A - parent) and the java compiler can infer this fact by its design.

Downcasting is the opposite:
    .......
    A a = new B(); //here you have an object from class B
                   //referenced to a variable of class A
                   //Implicit upcasting happens here.

    B b = (B) a; // this is downcasting.

Downcasting must be explicit. If you try to downcast an
object to a type that cant be downcasted to, then the compiler will offer protection by giving you an error.
Otherwise your program would have crashed at runtime.

You can test with the operator instanceOf whether a 
downcast is safe.
example:
   if (a instanceOf (B)) { 
        b = (B)a;
   }

The problem with this is that it creates a dependency in that segment of code and the type B. If in the future you create sibling to B classes you will have to remember to change all the places where you are checking with "instanceOf" and this can be a try nightmare on a large system.

So, generally speaking, you want to create code that avoids downcasting. This is not always possible and not always a good idea as there are many cases where explicit downcasting is needed or at least is possible to use without problems. 
Again generally speaking, you want to create designs that have parts that are as independent, decoupled, reusable, robust, modular, and change adaptive as possible. This is not easy because many of these forces lead you to contradictions. So its all about making choices.

